etter at  learning languages  i don't necessarily agree but but  younger children have certain advantages  number one they are not embarrassed they  are not concerned  they're not afraid to sound like  children when they speak because they  are children  um we uh people who are able to speak  very well in their own language are  reluctant to speak in a second language  because they feel that they  sound foolish they sound like children  they can't say what they want to say and  so forth so they have a lot of hang-ups  but  older learners bring with them all of  the knowledge the experience  the literacy they have  typically most often not always in their  own language uh i mean if now that i'm  learning russian i don't want to sound  like a five-year-old russian child or a  three-year-old russian child i want to  read tolstoy i want to read the daily  news i want eventually to be able to  discuss things as an adult  so um and and i will do that and i will  achieve it within two years  uh with an hour of study a day here and  there occasionally more  months of doing nothing  i will achieve it  um  child  you know  thrust in a situation where everyone  else is speaking another language  they'll learn that language very quickly  but they'll only learn it to the level  and to the vocabulary level of of  children of their age  so i think children there's two things  with children one is that they  obviously are less inhibited they're not  corrected they're not afraid to make  mistakes they're not afraid to  come across as a child because they are  a child  and the second thing is i believe that  probably although i'm not a  neuroscientist obviously but probably um  in the first five six seven years of  your life as the the you know the all  the neurons in your brain are are  charging around and competing for space  and trying to establish  uh room for different  functions and and and cognitive you know  capabilities  if you are um  put in a situation where you hear a  variety of languages that's a good thing  and i if i were doing language study in  schools i would not try and teach one  second language i would expose kids to  many languages a lot of listening and  reading forget the grammar and and open  their minds to the different sounds and  the different ways that there are of  saying things in different languages let  them listen to stories and all of that  is beneficial but i know people who were  exposed to a second language as children  and who did not end up being good  language learners i know people who did  not have that exposure and became good  language learners whatever your genetic  predisposition is  whatever the environment you grew up in  as a child is  you know your capability to learn  languages is what it is so forget about  it forget about it the question now is  what are you going to do  and i think there  the  the approach that i take i mean i'm an  adult i've learned most of my languages  as an adult  i think it's the one that works best for  adults in other words  first of all  understand  work with input  get to the point where you can  understand what is said you can  understand what is written  because  yeah adults don't want to be confronted  with a situation where they come across  as stupid where they're looking for  words and they can't find them where  they're in a class with 20 people and  they're all bumbling  in another language uh far better to  spend  six months  getting used to a language doing a lot  of listening to a lot of reading  accumulate vocabulary and then  as you're ready for it start  speaking so i think  i mean i recommend the same for both i  recommend the same for children the  children listen to stories i think  that there's not that much of a  difference i think the adult has  a number of advantages over the child in  everything except pronunciation i think  the child's mind is more flexible more  open to new sounds  uh the  the adult is more inhibited  uh is perhaps restricted by the sounds  the sound range that they've sort of  you know spent 20 30 40 years you know  getting used to  so the children will do better on  pronunciation typically  but other than that the adult knows more  concepts knows more vocabulary in their  own language has more experience  and all of this is relevant as they try  to associate what they know in their own  language to what they're trying to learn  in the new language adults are better  language learners  overall period there's no question i  mean if if i went to anyone and said  look in six months you've got you know  full time you go at it but if you  achieve a level where you can read the  newspaper and you can discuss  politics or art or history in this other  language if you do that i'll give you a  million dollars  adults are going to make it  and children wouldn't  three-year-olds would not achieve that  level  so um adults can be better language  learners they have some hang-ups  uh the best approach for adults is the  input approach in my opinion very  definitely  and avoid being put in a situation where  you're forced to produce the language uh  too early now as i said in my in my  little uh presentation on output yeah do  talk once or twice a week three times a  week whatever you're comfortable with  but don't think you're gonna talk your  way to fluency don't plan on spending  five hours a day talking spend most of  your time listening and reading and  accumulating vocabulary so really the  message for the adults is not very  different than what it is  for  children and now i put my glasses back  on and see if i can turn all this off  again  so where are we here  okay
 

